okay I have an anecdote for you that's  really all it is but it's an interesting  one in a sense about the failures of our  healthcare system although I know that  this is both number one relatively minor  in the grand scheme of things and that  I'm fortunate enough to be in a position  not to be crippled financially by what  I'm about to tell you and it's also not  a serious health issue okay so here's my  medical background on this particular  story years and years ago I was having  knee pain when I would run and when I  would bike and when I would play  basketball and my doctor said it seems  like it's more than just you know you're  not 19 anymore you should go and see a  podiatrist and see what's going on could  be related to your feet so like 10 years  ago or something I went to a podiatrist  and the podiatrist said yep your knees  and ankles are not correctly aligned we  need to correct your arches with some  orthotic in soles she made me the  insoles obviously in the United States  the insoles themselves were not covered  by insurance so I bought the insoles and  paid my copay for the podiatrist and  I've had those insoles for a year and  over time for many years over time the  insoles start to flatten out just from  the weight of the person wearing them  and I was starting to have knee pain  again so I wanted to go to a podiatrist  get a new evaluation find out if I need  new insoles and also have them look at  my feet i have something called greek  feet hilariously where the second toe is  longer than the big toe and it can  sometimes scrunch that second toe and  create some weird stuff on the front of  the foot and the nail so I wanted that  looked at as well okay so I made an  appointment with my podiatrist I did all  of the right things I have insurance the  insurance is a totally normal  middle-of-the-road what's called a  silver plan meaning that my specialist  co-pays are $40 this is with a big  insurer here in Massachusetts called  Tufts health plan I don't know if people  around the country know about Tufts  health plan it's a pretty big insurer  here so I do all the right things I call  my insurance I say does my plan cover  podiatry David yes it does great which  podiatrists does my insurance cover  here's a list  great do I need a referral from my  regular doctor to see a podiatrist  no you don't you just call the  podiatrist and make an appointment  fantastic  do I need pre-authorization from you my  insurance in order to have the visit  covered no you don't David you just go  if it's someone on our list we will  cover all but the copay perfect I make  the appointment I go the doctor sees me  for 10 minutes okay 10 minutes says  David your arches are flat so you need  new insoles we don't make insoles here  here's a prescription for how the insole  should be made go to this other place  the insurance is not going to cover the  insoles the insoles are going to be  three hundred and seventy-five dollars  but your toes look fine nothing to worry  about there with your greek feet perfect  I take my prescription  I pay my $40 copay and I'm on my way two  weeks go by and I get a bill for four  hundred and fourteen dollars from the  podiatrist now how could this be I paid  my $40 copay I didn't need  pre-authorization I didn't need a  referral podiatrist covered I went to a  podiatrist covered by my plan well it  turns out that podiatry is covered and I  didn't need a referral and I didn't need  pre-authorization but the diagnosis of  flat feet is not covered and that is how  I was diagnosed and therefore I owe four  hundred and fifty four dollars for the  visit - the $40 copay or four hundred  and fourteen dollars so I say wait hold  on a second I went in for knee pain how  should I know in advance what the  diagnosis is going to be my insurance  says that's simply not our problem so  anyway I was able to speak to someone  who said what you should do is call the  doctor and ask if this is definitely  wink-wink the right diagnosis now of  course someone at Tufts health plan  can't say David let me help you commit  insurance fraud call the doctor and ask  for a different diagnosis so that we  will cover them obviously they can't do  that but I'm reading between the lines  and they say this is the system we have  I would call the doctor's office and  explain the situation so I call the  doctor's office and I explain and they  say oh we're gonna get someone to talk  to the doctor  maybe the doctor can rebuild this some  their way maybe focusing on the Greek  feet part or some other diagnosis other  than flat feet so at this point again  fortunately this is more a curiosity for  me can i altom utley get this covered I  may well end up paying 375 for the  insoles 414 for the visit plus my $40  copay right that's 829 dollars in  addition to my monthly insurance premium  of four hundred and twenty-five dollars  a month to get insoles okay in other  countries insoles are a hundred bucks  and the podiatrists themselves makes  them and it would be a fraction of the  cost so understand that part of the  inefficiency here is look at the number  of people that are involved with this  ten minute specialist visit I spoke to  one insurance person before making the  visit appointment to figure out what  isn't what isn't covered I spoke to  another insurance person after I saw the  doctor I was then checked out at the  office by someone in the billing  department to pay for my copay I then  called and spoke to someone in the  finance office for the podiatrist who  then will go back and speak to the  doctor and the care supervisor were up  to seven six people being spoken to in  seven different interactions and they  may end up rebuilding this some other  way how much of the gap between the  hundred dollars this would have cost in  other countries up maybe 150 to the  hundred to the 829 that in total I'm  going to pay how much of that nearly  seven hundred dollar difference is  related to the fact that I spoke to six  different people about billing related  issues and again I'm lucky that this  isn't serious and that I can't afford it  if I end up having no choice but to pay  829 total for this ridiculous Fiasco  I'll be okay that's not the case for a  lot of people who can't afford an  unexpected $400 expense and then of  course there's the broader question of  why would a ten minute visit to a  podiatrist even be priced at four  hundred and fifty four dollars which is  again related to all of the this BS that  is part of the system inefficiencies  bureaucracy billing coding non  payment from people who don't have  insurance and ultimately are just going  to skip out on the bill it is truly a  perverse system and I don't want to make  my story seem any more important than it  is I tell it merely as an anecdote that  I have send me your thoughts if you have  a similar story please post it in the  comments for the YouTube video for this  segment I do want to hear from you about  it
 

